```

JavaScript:
```javascript
function sortTable(n) {
  var table, rows, switching, i, x, y, shouldSwitch, dir, switchcount = 0;
  table = document.getElementById("myTable");
  switching = true;
  dir = "asc";
  rows = table.rows;
  for (i = 1; i < rows.length - 1; i++) {
    shouldSwitch = false;
    x = rows[i].getElementsByTagName("TD")[n];
    y = rows[i + 1].getElementsByTagName("TD")[n];
    if (dir == "asc") {
      if (x.innerHTML.toLowerCase() > y.innerHTML.toLowerCase()) {
        shouldSwitch = true;
      }
    } else if (dir == "desc") {
      if (x.innerHTML.toLowerCase() < y.innerHTML.toLowerCase()) {
        shouldSwitch = true;
      }
    }
    if (shouldSwitch) {
      rows[i].parentNode.insertBefore(rows[i + 1], rows[i]);
      switching = true;
      switchcount ++;
    } else {
      if (switchcount == 0 && dir == "asc") {
        dir = "desc";
        switching = false;
      }
    }
  }
  if (switching) {
    sortTable(n);
  }
}
```

This example sorts the table alphanumerically by clicking the headers. The `sortTable` function takes an argument `n` which represents the column index to sort by. The sorting direction is determined by the `dir` variable, which can be either "asc" or "desc". When clicking the same header again, the sorting direction will be reversed.

You can customize the table data and column headers as needed. Keep in mind that this example only works for single-level sorting. If you need multi-level sorting, you may need to modify the code accordingly.

You can also use CSS to style the table and headers to match the Bootstrap design.

